            <!-- Company Number (dual-input — exactly one is visible per mode) -->
            <div class="flex flex-col field field-reserved required">
              <label for="company_id" class="font-semibold text-gray-700">
                <?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr(__("Company Number")) ?>
              </label>
              <!--
                  `:disabled` is the ONLY thing that locks this field. It used
                  to be set imperatively from company-name-payment.phtml, which
                  disabled it on every shipping sync and never reversed it — so
                  a company whose national identifier the search response
                  omitted left the buyer with a required field that was empty
                  AND uneditable. The lock is now derived state
                  (`manualMode || companyIdEntryRequired`, see
                  applyCompanyIdEditability()) with exactly one writer.

                  The greyed-out look is `input.company_id:disabled` in
                  custom.css, NOT a `:style` binding. Two reasons: CSP-friendly
                  Alpine does not EVALUATE an attribute expression at all — it
                  looks the whole string up as a key on the component, so an
                  object literal `:style="{ backgroundColor: … }"` is not
                  something it can compute (that lookup is also why the sibling
                  `x-show="!showManual"` resolves — against the `['!showManual']`
                  getter, not by negating anything). And a
                  STRING `:style` sets the whole style attribute, which is
                  where `x-show` writes `display: none`. Since the two
                  bindings re-run on their own dependencies, a state change
                  that re-ran only `:style` would drop the hidden mirror
                  input's `display: none` and reveal it.
              -->
              <!--
                  Additive (TWO-25288): once `companyIdDisabled` is true the
                  number is locked AND now shown via the inline hint next to
                  the company name field above, so the input itself is
                  redundant — hide it with a class layered on top of the
                  existing `x-show="!showManual"` mechanism. `companyIdDisabled`
                  can only be true when NOT in manual mode (see
                  applyCompanyIdEditability()), so this never hides the field
                  while the buyer still needs to interact with it (empty,
                  enabled, required). Does not touch the existing `x-show` or
                  `:disabled` bindings. `:class` binds to a getter returning a
                  plain class-name string (`companyIdHiddenClass`), matching
                  this file's other `:class` bindings (`chipClasses`,
                  GetItemClass) — the CSP-friendly Alpine build here does not
                  evaluate object-literal expressions, only looks a bound
                  string up as a key on the component (see the `:style` note
                  below).
              -->
              <input
                type="text"
                x-show="!showManual"
                :class="companyIdHiddenClass"
                :id="<?= $gwBase ?>GetCompanyIdFieldId"
                :name="<?= $gwBase ?>GetCompanyIdFieldName"
                :disabled="companyIdDisabled"
                data-name="company_id"
                required
                data-validate='{"required": true}'
                class="company_id border border-gray-300 rounded-md p-2 focus:ring focus:ring-indigo-300 validate"
              />
              <input
                type="text"
                x-show="showManual"
                :id="<?= $gwBase ?>GetManualCompanyIdFieldId"
                :name="<?= $gwBase ?>GetManualCompanyIdFieldName"
                required
                data-validate='{"required": true}'
                class="company_id border border-gray-300 rounded-md p-2 focus:ring focus:ring-indigo-300 validate"
              />
